  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quasi stationary high pres sprawling across the\nMid- Atlantic and low pressure well off the FL coast will \nresult in cont northeasterly flow across the region today. The \nhigh waves combined with King Tides this week will lead to \nadditional minor coastal flooding impacts on the OBX.\n\nFor Hatteras Island, seas offshore are in the ~6 foot range, \nbut the long period swells will continue to cause issues for \nvulnerable areas. For ocean-side: high tide is around 0900 AM \nEDT today. The National Parks Service and NCDOT have reported \nsubstantial beach erosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along \nwith significant overwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe.\nDue to hazardous road conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains \nclosed from the Marc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe. \n\nMinor coastal flooding of vulnerable locations will be likely \nwell into this week despite the diminishing of winds, with \n\"sunny day\" flooding across much of the tidal regions of \ncoastal ENC around times of high tide, then slowly improve as \nthe King Tides ease later in the week and into the weekend. \nCoastal flood advisories continue through the high tide cycle on\nThursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Quasi stationary dry high pres cont to sprawl \nacross the region. Weak coastal troughing offshore could bring \na few showersto mainly OBX/Downeast today, but most areas \nremain rain-free, albeit with mo cloudy skies today. Late week \ninto the weekend, hts/thicknesses build further, and temps \nclimbing back to ~10 degrees above climo with highs upr 70s to \nnear 80 inland to low 70s OBX. Low temps will warm above climo \nas well with readings in the mid 50s for most locales away from \nthe immediate coast. \n\nHigh pres ridge may begin to break down as early as Sunday, but\nrecent runs from EC/AIFS cont to keep a stronger high in place.\nMay have to wait until a stronger srn stream system sweeps\nacross the Deep South and into the region later in the week to\nsee next good chance for appreciable rain. For now, pops remain\nquite low for late Sun into Mon ~30% to account for the small\nshower chances.",
